Merck appears to be the lumpier earner. Its quarterly net income moves from profits such as 5.785 billion and 4.427 billion to losses of 4.240 billion and 1.335 billion in the more recent quarters; the earlier series also includes a loss of 5.975 billion followed by profits above 5 billion. Lilly’s earnings have grown sharply, but its recent pattern is comparatively consistent: 6.638 billion, 7.396 billion, and 7.095 billion across the latest three quarters shown. Lilly did have a notably weak quarter at 970.3 million in 2024, so “smoother” should not be confused with immune to discrete charges.

The likely distinction is not simply product-sales seasonality. Large pharmaceutical net-income swings are usually driven by items below, or outside, recurring product demand: acquired in-process research and development, business-development and licensing payments, asset impairments, litigation or tax settlements, restructuring, changes in the value of equity investments, and tax-rate changes. A large charge can turn a fundamentally profitable drug franchise into a reported quarterly loss, while leaving revenue and operating demand far less volatile. Merck’s profit-to-loss reversals are therefore a strong signal to look for such nonrecurring or mark-to-market items; the supplied net-income data alone cannot establish which one caused them.

To confirm the diagnosis, obtain each relevant 10-Q and 10-K and reconcile net income to operating income and then to pretax income. In particular, review:

- the condensed income statement for revenue, gross margin, R&D, acquired IPR&D, selling and administrative expense, other income/expense, and income taxes;
- MD&A’s quarter-over-quarter and year-over-year explanations, including management’s description of unusual expenses or gains;
- notes on acquisitions, collaborations, licensing arrangements, contingent consideration, impairments, restructuring, litigation, investments, and taxes;
- segment and product-sales tables, to determine whether the volatility began in sales or arose below operating profit;
- the cash-flow statement, because a large noncash impairment or fair-value loss can depress earnings without a comparable cash outflow; and
- the effective-tax-rate reconciliation and uncertain-tax-position disclosures.

One data-quality point also matters: the fourth-quarter figures shown are derived as fiscal-year net income less nine-month net income. They are useful for comparison, but the filed annual report should be used to verify the underlying components. The key test is straightforward: if Merck’s revenue and core operating profit remain relatively stable while “other,” IPR&D, impairment, or tax lines swing sharply, its lumpiness is accounting- and transaction-driven rather than a deterioration in the underlying franchise.
